
Subject: GNU Mes 0.28 released

<#secure method=pgpmime mode=sign>
We are happy to announce the release of GNU Mes 0.28.

Mes 0.28 is a big change. It represents hundreds of commits during
around a year and a half happening in parallel with the main release
cycle, but that have been reduced and cleaned up to 58 commits since
0.27.2.  This release reimplements the core of the Scheme interpreter
moving from a Tree-Walk interpreter to a Bytecode compiler and
interpreter, and implements the Macro Expansion directly in Scheme,
achieving a substantial performance increase.  It also includes a few
cleanups and bugfixes.

See the NEWS below for a brief summary.

We are most grateful for NLnet sponsoring this work.

Thanks to everyone who has contributed!
The following people contributed changes to this release:

  Ekaitz Zarraga (34)
  Janneke Nieuwenhuizen (4)
  Timothy Sample (20)

What's next?

Speeding up the build system using a unity build.  Adding support for
for bignums.  Integrating Popsyntax, a fully-hygienic macro expander.
Also, lots of performance improvements.

Enjoy!

Ekaitz
 [on behalf of the mes maintainers]


ABOUT

GNU Mes is a Scheme interpreter and C compiler for bootstrapping the
GNU System.  It has helped to decimate the number and size of binary
seeds that were used in the bootstrap of GNU Guix 1.0.  Recently,
version 0.24.2 has realized the first Full Source Bootstrap for Guix
<https://guix.gnu.org/en/blog/2023/the-full-source-bootstrap-building-from-source-all-the-way-down/>.
The final goal is to help create a full source bootstrap as part of
the bootstrappable builds effort <https://bootstrappable.org> for any
UNIX-like operating system.

Mes + MesCC + Mes C Library can build a bootstrappable TinyCC
<https://gitlab.com/janneke/tinycc> that is self-hosting.  Using this
bootstrappable-tcc and the Mes C library we can build gcc-4.6.  This is
enough to bootstrap Guix for i686-linux, x86_64-linux, armhf-linux,
aarch64-linux, and riscv64-linux.

Mes was inspired by The Maxwell Equations of Software: LISP-1.5 --
John McCarthy page 13, Guix's source/binary packaging transparency and
Jeremiah Orians's Stage0 <https://github.com/oriansj/stage0>, a
~300-byte self-hosting hex assembler.
